# Horse Model-First API Cleanup

## Goal

Horse exposes model loading as the normal way for game code to create rendered
geometry. Raw mesh construction and renderer/backend plumbing remain available
inside Horse, but are no longer part of the normal public API.

## Public geometry contract

Game code loads an asset with `Models::load()` and creates it in an ECS world
with `Renderer::ModelScene::instantiate()`. Placement and behavior are then
controlled through generic ECS state such as `Transform`, `Parent`,
`RenderLayerComponent`, lights, environment, animation and LOD.

The supported flow is:

    asset file -> Models::load -> validated model data
               -> ModelScene::instantiate -> ECS entities
               -> one renderer geometry pipeline

There is no separate renderer for imported models. Importers still normalize
their data into Horse's internal mesh representation, and every renderer uses
that representation.

## Internal geometry contract

The following are Horse implementation details rather than gameplay APIs:

- mesh/material registry writes (`registerMesh`, `updateMesh`,
  `registerMaterial`, `updateMaterial`)
- renderer-only mesh/instance/renderable ECS components
- model deformation state used by skinning and morph targets
- model-scene animation pointer plumbing
- shared shading-state storage
- window backend access and the UI backend render pass

Read-only model inspection remains available where it is useful to animation,
debugging and tooling. No compatibility wrappers are required for the removed
low-level mutation APIs.

## API cleanup boundaries

This cleanup removes clear implementation leaks and redundant aliases while
preserving generic engine features. `Renderer::Manager`, the renderer classes,
`Models::Runtime`, model metadata inspection, debug rendering, physics,
post-processing and renderer switching remain supported.

`Camera::Controller` is removed because it is only an alias for
`Camera::FreeController`.

Internal headers are placed under an `Internal` path/namespace where practical,
without changing behavior merely for naming consistency.

## GAME integration

GAME no longer constructs a cube/pyramid/floor by hand. `TestScene.cpp` and
`TestScene.hpp` are removed. The world scene is
`Assets/Sponza/sponza.obj`, loaded with `Models::load()` and instantiated with
`Renderer::ModelScene::instantiate()` under one scaled root transform.

The Sponza source bounds are approximately 3720.85 x 1555.88 x 2288.23, so a
0.01 root scale gives a practical 37.2 x 15.6 x 22.9 world. The root is raised
by about 1.264 units so the lowest source vertex is near y=0 after scaling.

The existing arms/revolver viewmodel stays on `RenderLayer::Overlay` and keeps
its separate camera-depth pass.

## Verification

Completion requires:

- Horse builds cleanly after public/internal API changes.
- a compile probe using only normal public model APIs can load and instantiate
  Sponza without raw mesh registration calls.
- GAME builds against the cleaned Horse API.
- GAME starts with Sponza loaded and the rasterizer initialized.
- no GAME source uses `MeshData`, `registerMesh`, `registerMaterial`,
  `updateMesh`, `updateMaterial`, `MeshComponent`, `InstanceComponent` or
  `RenderableComponent` directly.
- the raw registry write functions are absent from `Models/Models.hpp`.
- the existing overlay/viewmodel render-layer behavior remains intact.

